  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unusually warm weather is expected this Tuesday and \nWednesday, with forecast temperatures potentially reaching record \nhighs for early November.\n\nA strong upper level ridge will build across the eastern CONUS \nthrough mid-week. Surface high pressure will persist across the \neastern seaboard and maintain mostly dry weather. During this time, \nwarm air advection will strengthen across the region. Isolated \nsprinkles can't be ruled out today. Weak isentropic lift and \nincreasing moisture will support widely scattered showers and cloud \ncover across western NY late tonight through Tuesday morning, and \nacross north-central NY late Tuesday morning through Tuesday \nafternoon. Drier air will move back into the region from west to \neast Tuesday afternoon through Tuesday night.  \n\nWhile clouds and a few showers impact the region Tuesday, 850mb \ntemperatures are expected to reach +11C. Max temperatures are \nexpected to reach low 70s across western NY and the mid to upper 60s \nacross north-central NY. The upper level ridge is expected to build \nfurther north with 850mb temperatures climbing to +13C on Wednesday. \nAnother very warm day for early November is expected with maximum \ntemperatures reaching the mid to upper 70s. Forecast southerly winds \nwill likely limit lake breeze influences across the region. This \nsetup translates to a very unusual event for the region for early \nNovember according to the ECMWF EFI. See Climate section for more \ninformation on record temperatures. \n\nA 25-30 knot low level jet is expected to move into the western \nSouthern Tier Tuesday night. A south-southeast surface wind and \nmodest inversion may support gusty downslope winds up to 30 mph \nalong and to the lee of the Chautauqua Ridge. Gusts will lessen in \nthis area Wednesday. \n\nCooler air will build into the region from west to east Thursday, \nhowever it will remain warm especially from the Finger Lakes region \ninto north-central NY. Cooler weather, although still above normal \nis expected Friday into the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There is high probability of widespread showers as a \ncold front arrives Wednesday night through Thursday. Unsettled \nweather is expected to continue into the weekend.  \n\nA deep trough and closed low will move into the Great Lakes region \nlate in the week. A deep southerly flow originating from the \nGulf and southern Atlantic Ocean will send a plume of moisture \ninto the eastern Great Lakes region Wednesday night through \nThursday morning. Forcing from the surface to 500mb and deep \nmoisture will support high confidence for showers across the \nregion. This will provide some relief to the recent dry spell, \nhowever the residence time of showers will be short-lived as the\nplume of moisture moves east of the region by Thursday \nafternoon. Forecast rainfall amounts are less than 0.25\" across \nwestern NY, even less across the Genesee Valley and Finger \nLakes region, and 0.25-0.50\" across north-central NY with the \nhighest amounts expected across the Tug Hill region. \n\nThe trough will move overhead Friday through Saturday. A cold front \nis expected to move across the region and showers will be possible \nthrough this time.",
